carried over 8. 5+9 is 1, plus 3 is 1. So that's 6 here, no carryover.\n\nFinally, the billions place: The first number has a 6, and the second has nothing, so just 6. Adding any carryover? No, since there was none from the previous step. So that's 7 in the billions place.\n\nPutting all the digits together from right to left, the sum should be... let me check again step by step to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nWait, maybe I should write it out vertically to visualize better:\n\n      125,167,496\n    + 463,424,656\n    ----------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes: 5 +1 =5\nTens:9+9=07 (carry 3)\nHundreds:9+8+9=39 (carry 3)\nThousands:9+4+7=46 (carry 3)\nTen thousands:2+2+0=0 (no carry)\nHundred thousands:5+7=22 (carry 9)\nMillions:0+4+5=3 (no carry)\nTen millions:0+1=53 (carry 2)\nHundred millions:8+7+8=3\nBillions:9\n\nWait, hold on. Let me verify each step again carefully. Maybe I missed a place.\n\nBreaking down each digit position with their place values:\n\nLet me align both numbers properly:\n\nFirst number: 1 2 8 3 2 7 9 0 4\n\nSecond number: 2 0 2 3 2 4 9 4 3\n\nWait, actually, let's count the digits. 624,256,708 has 7 digits: 6 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 4 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 2 (ones).\n\nThe second number, 269,284,920 is also 8 digits: 9 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 5 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 6 (ones).\n\nWait, maybe I miscounted. Let me write each digit's place:\n\nFirst number: 8 (billions place? Wait, 188,567,949. Let's parse the commas. In some notations, commas separate every three digits. So 747,494,765 is 5,485,186,85? Wait, no. Wait, actually, 064,492,009 would be 0 hundred fifty-one million... Wait, actually, commas in numbers can vary by region, but assuming standard US notation where each comma separates thousands, millions, etc. So 282,520,789 is 662 million, 170 thousa